# Domain III-C: Dose-Response — Source Map & Key Page Ranges

Compiled from a 200-question synthetic generation session (2026-05-30). Intended as quick-reference for future Domain III-C question generation sessions.

## Source Documents

### 1. Casarett & Doull's Toxicology, 9th Ed — Ch.4 "Risk Assessment" (pp.146-175)
- **pp.162-163**: NOAEL/LOAEL definitions, threshold dose-response, Fig.4-5 illustration, POD concept
- **pp.163**: RfD = NOAEL/(UF×MF) formula, standard UFs (UFA=10, UFH=10, UFS=10, UFL=10), composite 100-fold factor, FQPA children's safety factor
- **pp.164**: MOE = NOAEL/human exposure, NOAEL criticisms (must be tested dose, ignores curve shape), MOE < 100 flag, Renwick/IPCS UF subdivision (TK 4.0, TD 2.5)
- **pp.164-165**: BMD method (Crump 1980), BMDL as 95% lower bound, advantages over NOAEL
- **pp.165**: BMDS software (EPA v2.7+), BMR at 1%/5%/10%, BMD applied to developmental toxicity
- **pp.165-166**: Nonthreshold models, one-hit model: P(d) = 1-exp(-λd), Armitage-Doll multistage model, LMS model (linear at low doses), q₁* calculation
- **pp.166**: BBDR models (MVK two-stage), PBPK models, hormesis (U-shaped curves)
- **pp.167**: Cancer slope factor = q₁*, unit risk (risk per μg/m³ or μg/L), VSD = lower 95% CL on de minimis dose, IRIS program

### 2. Hayes' Principles & Methods of Toxicology, 7th Ed — Ch.3 "Dose-Response" (pp.125-172)
- **p.96**: Dose-response as most fundamental concept in toxicology (Calabrese thesis)
- **pp.98-105**: Historical debate — Paracelsus, Hahnemann/homeopathy, Hugo Schulz, A.J. Clark, Arndt-Schulz Law, biphasic dose-response marginalization
- **pp.105-107**: Threshold model origins in occupational radiation protection (ICRP 1928, Lauriston Taylor)
- **pp.107-112**: Theoretical foundations: DNA adduct threshold concept, dose-dependent transitions (saturation of detoxification), thermodynamic basis
- **pp.112-113**: Plotting data — threshold vs linearity debate
- **pp.114-117**: LNT model and ionizing radiation, population heterogeneity-based LNT
- **pp.117-124**: Hormesis: biphasic J-shaped/U-shaped, Calabrese database, modest stimulation (30-60% above control), Southam & Ehrlich coined term
- **pp.122-124**: ED01 Mega-Mouse Study (24,000 mice, 2-AAF), Branham's replication of Schulz (time-dependent overcompensation)
- **pp.126-127**: Pharmacological biphasic responses (clinical implications)
- **pp.128-131**: Dose-time-response vs dose-response, adaptive response/preconditioning, high-risk groups, triphasic responses
- **pp.130-131**: MTD concerns, shallow/steep curves, cumulative dose-response vs dose rate
- **p.131**: Carcinogen latency, epidemiology and dose-response

### 3. EPA Cancer Guidelines (2005) — Section 3 "Dose-Response Assessment"
- **§3.1.1-3.1.3**: Standardizing dosing regimens, toxicokinetic data/PBPK, cross-species scaling (BW^(3/4), body surface area, RDDR for inhalation)
- **§3.1.4**: Route-to-route extrapolation
- **§3.2.1-3.2.3**: Epidemiologic studies, toxicodynamic (BBDR) modeling, empirical curve fitting (BMD)
- **§3.2.4-3.2.5**: POD selection (BMDL10 preferred), POD narrative
- **§3.3.1**: Choosing linear vs nonlinear extrapolation based on MOA
- **§3.3.2-3.3.3**: Toxicodynamic model extrapolation, low-dose linear model
- **§3.3.4**: Nonlinear extrapolation (RfD/RfC approach for threshold MOA)
- **§3.3.5**: Comparing and combining multiple extrapolations
- **§3.6-3.7**: Uncertainty, dose-response characterization

### 4. Silver Book (NRC 2009)
- **Ch.2**: Current EPA practices (IRIS, RfD/RfC, slope factors)
- **Ch.5**: Key dose-response advances (BMD, PBPK, cumulative risk)
- **Ch.7**: Selection and use of default assumptions
- **Ch.8**: Three-phase framework

## Key Numeric Constants

| Parameter | Value | Source |
|-----------|-------|--------|
| Default interspecies UF (UFA) | 10 (TK=4.0, TD=2.5) | C&D Ch.4 p.163 |
| Default intraspecies UF (UFH) | 10 (TK=3.16, TD=3.16) | C&D Ch.4 p.164 |
| Subchronic-to-chronic UF (UFS) | 10 | C&D Ch.4 p.163 |
| LOAEL-to-NOAEL UF (UFL) | 10 | C&D Ch.4 p.163 |
| Composite default (animal→human) | 100 (10×10) | C&D Ch.4 p.163 |
| FQPA children's safety factor | 10 | C&D Ch.4 p.163 (FQPA PL 104-170) |
| De minimis cancer risk range | 10⁻⁴ to 10⁻⁶ | C&D Ch.4 p.166 |
| Default BMR for cancer | 10% (BMD10/BMDL10) | EPA Cancer Guidelines §3.2.4 |
| Default BMR for developmental | 5% (BMD05) | C&D Ch.4 p.165 |
| Acceptable MOE threshold | < 100 flags further review | C&D Ch.4 p.164 |
| Hormetic max stimulation | 30-60% above control | Hayes Ch.3 p.121 |
| NOAEL avg risk (continuous) | ~5% | C&D Ch.4 p.163 |
| NOAEL risk (quantal) | >10% possible | C&D Ch.4 p.163 |
